 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering time is constant with respect to volume resolution, as only one primitive (36 indices) is drawn per frame [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is constant and minimal, storing only 8 vertices and 36 indices regardless of the underlying volume data size [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Extremely simple to implement; minimal GPU memory footprint for geometry; suitable for debugging or low-resolution previews [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Cannot cull empty space; performance does not scale with volume size but visual fidelity is limited by the single-cube approximation; requires the shader to handle the entire volume sampling [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **When to use:** Simple volume visualization where performance is critical and volume is small or fully opaque [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Large volumes with significant empty space, or when high-resolution internal structure visualization is required without shader-based optimization [S2].
+Time and memory complexity are constant with respect to volume resolution, as only one primitive (36 indices) is submitted per frame [S1, unofficial]. The method requires the shader to handle the entire volume sampling [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the SGI OpenGL Volumizer (vz) library, specifically the `vzBlock` class, which provides a simple axis-aligned cube geometry for volume rendering applications [S2]. It is part of the early 2000s SGI technology stack for scientific visualization [S2]. Modern implementations, such as the unofficial medical volume renderer, adapt this concept using standard OpenGL/Vulkan pipelines [S1, S1, unofficial].
